data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":1705,"item_id":"msg_0c3fce3dbe6b7ef6016b0327e77f2487d1bf450563674522b6","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\n  \"reviews\": [\n    {\n      \"actor\": \"birch-3\",\n      \"decision\": \"develop\",\n      \"ability\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"Existing root-preparation supplies the crushing operation, costs and food-accounting rules, but its fixed one-unit bark prerequisite cannot assess or use a physically sufficient fractional piece. Develop one shared fractional-surface extension for both applicants, reusing that implementation rather than creating another crushing function. The reported failure establishes a quantity restriction, not physical insufficiency. Ownership alone does not establish that the implements are currently reachable.\",\n      \"specification\": \"Implement root-preparation@2 with assess-surface and prepare-small operations, retaining the existing interface and legacy operations. Public schema: {operation, barkId, toolId, rootId?, quantity?:0.1..1 in actual supported increments, site?:{x,y}, processId?}; status/cancel require processId. Shared reference parameters: interaction reach 0.65, existing world collision rules, bark density default 500 kg/m3 and thickness 0.003 m only where unrepresented, one-root working footprint 0.16 by 0.16 m including containment margin. Existing measured geometry overrides defaults. Represent per-piece mass, dimensions, connectedness, holes, condition, source provenance, assessment evidence and model assumptions. A single extraction-derived bark lot may receive a mass-conserving rectangular-sheet fallback model with aspect ratio 1.5 during paid assessment, unless its retained history records fragmentation; preserve explicit uncertainty and distinguish model-derived dimensions from observations. Stack combining never joins sheets; fines are not a continuous surface. Do not multiply a per-unit profile by quantity twice: these 0.3 bark units normally represent 0.09 mass, subject to authoritative retained accounting. Assessment takes one round and 0.2 energy with the actual bark and stone accessible; check intact usable rectangle, flexibility, stone hardness at least 3, and a clear level support site. Report measured/modelled dimensions and exact unmet conditions; reserve no roots and grant no success merely from the supplied IDs. A suitable bark sheet contains food on firm ground; it is not a freestanding impact support. Preparation reserves only the selected usable sheet, reusable stone and at most one actual root; split the selected root quantity out of its stack and leave the other root untouched. Reuse root-preparation crushing behavior: two active work-round equivalents and two energy per root, preserving mass, nutrients, toxicity, age and provenance; resulting crushed material ages at 1.25 baseline and gains no nutrition, safety or preservation benefit. Each paid round requires a living actor, sufficient energy, reachable implements and an unobstructed work patch in shared geometry. Store owner, site, exact escrow IDs, workDone and workRemaining; progress changes only after paid work. Inaccessible conditions pause without work or energy debit while food ages; explicit cancellation and death terminate, releasing the same implements and the actual partially processed material at the site without cloning, deletion or work reset. Completion releases implements and the real output once, with idempotent event delivery. Declare root-surface-assessed and root-preparation-stopped; retain food-preparation-started, process-progress, food-preparation-completed and information-confirmed bindings. Event payloads include participant, source IDs, geometry basis, costs, progress, outputs and failure reasons. Migrate existing bark histories lazily without adding material or declaring them suitable; preserve active legacy processes and satisfaction equivalence keys. Reuse crushing, inventory, collision, provenance and feedback code explicitly; the new independently learnable content is surface assessment and fractional setup, requiring root-preparation mastery. Individual extension study is one round; implementation and review advance no simulation time.\",\n      \"experienceReview\": \"A successful paid assessment or completed trial can confirm previously unknown surface suitability; application, study, a quantity-check failure and repeated known results cannot. Bind qualified information-confirmed events from root-preparation to the existing grounded-understanding source, not a new alias: participant is the actual assessor or experimenter, evidence must establish new personally unconfirmed information from the real inspected piece or trial. Retain amount 0.8, cap 1, hold duration 8, refresh repetition and cooldown 12; maintained gain 0.035 per qualifying event with decay 0.0015, sharing the existing source's bounded saturation and cooldown across interfaces. Crushing alone supplies no additional pleasure or maintained satisfaction, and mastery earns nothing. Existing practice rules may apply only under their existing qualification contract, without duplicate awards. Physical benefits remain actual later consumption and processing effects, not immediate comfort, healing or happiness.\",\n      \"visualReview\": \"Show the actual sheet and stone at the validated work patch, with the selected root quantity and partial crushing state. Inspection displays usable dimensions, continuity, model-versus-measurement basis and the footprint test. Display remaining work, paid energy, reservation ownership and specific pause/failure conditions. Replay binds to the declared assessment, progress, completion and stop events, preserving source/output identities; it must never depict the unreserved second root as processed or fines as an assembled sheet.\",\n      \"learning\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"actor\": \"birch-5\",\n      \"decision\": \"develop\",\n      \"ability\": null,\n      \"reason\": \"The same missing fractional-surface assessment affects this request. Existing probe can inspect and existing root-preparation can crush, but neither currently connects a physically sufficient fractional sheet to executable preparation. Use the single shared root-preparation@2 development above, not a second implementation. No root is required for assessment; preparing food later requires an actual owned root. Fines cannot substitute for a connected sheet merely by adding their mass.\",\n      \"specification\": \"Co-develop the identical root-preparation@2 contract specified for birch-3, including its public schema, shared physical parameters, conservative geometry model, event declarations, conserved escrow, progress/pause/completion/death-cancellation behavior, provenance and migration. Apply assess-surface to the explicitly selected bark and stone only after validating actual ownership, accessibility, physical profiles and a clear supporting site. Assessment costs one round and 0.2 energy and returns the usable connected rectangle, assumptions and exact sufficiency result. Do not consume the fines or manufacture an assembled sheet; this smallest version implements assessment and single-sheet preparation, not unsupported joining. If the sheet fails, report the missing dimensions or integrity rather than granting suitability. No root is reserved during assessment. prepare-small accepts an actual selected owned root quantity of at most one unit, costs two work-round equivalents and two energy per root, and otherwise uses the shared contract unchanged. Status exposes process owner, remaining work, escrow and geometry; cancellation or death leaves actual tools and partial food at the site exactly once. Reuse existing crushing and inspection infrastructure as code reuse, while recording fractional setup as the newly developed functionality. This applicant separately studies the extension for one round because root-preparation is already mastered; no mastery, materials, completed assessment or elapsed simulation time is supplied by development.\",\n      \"experienceReview\": \"Use precisely the shared grounded-understanding subscription described for birch-3: qualified information-confirmed from an actual paid assessment or experiment, benefiting only its actual participant when it establishes personally new information. Amount 0.8, cap 1, duration 8, refresh with cooldown 12; maintained gain 0.035 and decay 0.0015 under the existing shared source cap. Observing another actor's nominal-unit rejection does not establish this sheet's physical suitability. Rechecking a known finding, requesting, learning and labels earn nothing. There is no separate pleasure source for assessing bark or achieving mastery, and no automatic physical comfort, nutrition or food-safety effect.\",\n      \"visualReview\": \"Use the shared surface-assessment and preparation presentation: highlight the actual selected sheet and stone, show connected usable dimensions and the required footprint, and mark assumptions separately from observations. Show fines as separate loose material, never a fabricated continuous surface. Bind observable costs, inspection results, pauses, remaining work and actual outputs to root-surface-assessed, process-progress, food-preparation-completed and root-preparation-stopped, with stable IDs for replay. Without an actual root and completed work, display no prepared food.\",\n      \"learning\": 1\n    }\n  ]\n}","logprobs":[]}

